      "schema:termCode": "compound_endocannabinoid"
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:DefinedTerm",
      "@id": "https://japan-cannabinoid-trends.routeflags.com/taxonomy/compound_identity_unverified",
      "schema:name": "Identity-Unverified Registered Terms (registration state)",
      "schema:inDefinedTermSet": {
        "@type": "schema:DefinedTermSet",
        "@id": "https://japan-cannabinoid-trends.routeflags.com/taxonomy/compound-identity-unverified-scheme"
      },
      "schema:alternateName": "同一性未確認の登録語（登録状態）",
      "schema:description": "An ADMINISTRATIVE registration-state category, not a route/origin or chemical classification. Its members are search terms the owner named for this study whose single-substance chemical identity is UNCONFIRMED in this repository. Placing a term here records only that the string is registered and queryable: it makes no claim about route or origin, natural occurrence, chemical class, structure, synonym relationships or regulatory status, and it neither asserts that the term names a distinct substance nor that a substance behind the string exists. Membership is not a judgement that a term is rare, minor, semi-synthetic or synthetic, and it never substitutes for one of those categories; when evidence arrives, move the term to the category that evidence supports. No identifier property and no `skos:exactMatch` are assigned to a member's taxonomy concept, and no chemical-class link is asserted; CAS / PubChem / UNII / ChEBI / Wikidata / IUPAC values quoted in a member's scopeNote are recorded evidence in `docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md`, not identifier properties, and per-isomer registry records for a family-scoped member belong in the provenance ledger, never on the concept. Membership itself establishes no synonym relationship. A member's skos:altLabel is a concept-level record: an alternate or search-topic label that the owner confirmed needs its own recorded basis in that concept's scopeNote (for example an owner-confirmed intended meaning), and it is neither implied by, nor does it add a synonym claim to, this administrative placement. For a family-scoped member such a label does not assert that the family denotes one chemical structure. A term is registered here because the owner named it, not because its identity was established. Decision record: ISS-20261111-TAX-040, which supersedes the ISS-20261015-TAX-027 non-registration decision for THCP. A member may also be a term the owner confirmed only as an isomer-unspecified homologue family, or as an isomer-unspecified ester (acetate) of one: the term then denotes no single substance and no single external record, so it stays here and its own scopeNote records the family scope; that statement is the term's documented search scope, not a route/origin placement, not a chemical-class link, and not a claim about any individual result. A family-scoped member's scopeNote may also record that its per-isomer substances fall within a documented statutory structural group; that is a statement about those isomers, not a regulatory classification performed by this placement. A member may also be a specific positional-isomer designation (for example a Δ8 or Δ9 term) that the owner confirmed as a search topic, where this change did not verify a single external registry record for the registered string: the term then records the search topic and the published nomenclature, while the category still makes no chemical claim and assigns no identifier property. This batch also registered a specifically named stereochemical configuration whose only recorded basis is a nomenclature or WHO review of possible configurations, with no per-configuration registry record and no product identification, and an isomer-unspecified acetate of a homologue family whose individual positional structures the repository does not enumerate: in both cases the concept records the search topic and the published nomenclature, and this category still makes no chemical, route, regulatory or product claim.",
      "schema:termCode": "compound_identity_unverified"
    },
