      "schema:alternateName": [
        "THCP-O-acetate"
      ],
      "schema:description": "'THCPO' is the owner's term, registered as a search string on 2026-11-11 by owner direction (ISS-20261111-TAX-040). On 2026-11-11 the owner answered `y` to the confirmation question 'Does `THCPO` mean THCP-O-acetate?' and named no Δ8 or Δ9 isomer. The registration is therefore treated as an isomer-unspecified THCP-O-acetate family: that family scope is this repository's registration-level reading of the answer, not a family the owner explicitly confirmed. Δ8-THCP-O-acetate and Δ9-THCP-O-acetate are separate substances, the registered string identifies neither isomer, and no Google Trends or YouTube result is attributed to an isomer. The English `skos:altLabel` 'THCP-O-acetate' records the owner-confirmed intended meaning as a search-scope label; its basis is this scopeNote, it is not implied by the administrative category's membership, and it is not a claim that the family is one chemical structure. No relation is asserted to `compound_thc_o` (a separately registered market/family concept with its own identity caveat, to which no single verified structure is assigned here), to THCP-O-butanoate (a different ester that Tanaka et al. 2025 identified alongside the Δ9 acetate), or to `compound_hhcpo` (a different registered term whose chemical identity is not established). No single external record denotes the family: chemical registries hold per-isomer records (one for the Δ9 acetate and one for the Δ8 acetate), the Wikidata item labelled 'THCP-O-acetate' is keyed to the Δ9 record, the recorded ChEBI search for the term `THCP` returned no match, and an FDA GSRS name search for `Tetrahydrocannabiphorol` returned only the parent record (UNII WTK87HN3LG); neither search established a THCP-O-acetate record. Therefore this concept carries no skos:exactMatch and no CAS number, PubChem CID, UNII, ChEBI ID or Wikidata QID: the per-isomer records are evidence about the isomers, documented in docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md, never this concept's identifier. The single-substance chemical identity remains UNCONFIRMED and the term stays in compound_identity_unverified, an administrative registration-state category, so this placement asserts no route/origin, natural occurrence, chemical class, pharmacology, safety or synonym relationship. Regulatory status of the two isomers: the 2024 MHLW amendment (令和6年厚生労働省令第81号, promulgated 2024-05-01, in force 2024-05-11) defines generic structural groups that include the C1-acetoxy, C3 straight-chain C7 member in both the 6a,7,8,10a-tetrahydro (Δ9) and the 6a,7,10,10a-tetrahydro (Δ8) series, subject to the ordinance's exclusion of narcotics (麻薬を除く). That is a statutory structural fact about those structures under that exclusion; it is not a classification of any product or search result, not a regulatory determination made here, and not part of the owner's identity confirmation. Tanaka et al. 2025 (DOI 10.1002/dta.3883, PMID 40075559) identified Δ9-THCP-O (acetate) and Δ9-THCP-O-butanoate in seven oil products and one herbal product distributed online, and also Δ8-THC-O (acetate), the C5 analogue, which is not Δ8-THCP-O-acetate; that supports the Δ9 acetate and the butanoate as analysed substances, not a Δ8 THCP acetate and not the content of any search result. **Query limitation:** Google Trends and YouTube series for `THCPO` / `THCPO リキッド` measure occurrences of those literal strings; `THCPO` is an acronym that can match unrelated text, and a matching result is not evidence of THCP-O-acetate content. Evidence that would move the term out of this category: an owner-confirmed single substance with a verified external identifier, or a recorded source naming a single substance and its route/origin; the basis belongs in docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md.",
      "schema:termCode": "compound_thcpo"
    },
    {
      "@type": "schema:DefinedTerm",
      "@id": "https://japan-cannabinoid-trends.routeflags.com/taxonomy/compound_cbe",
      "schema:name": "Cannabielsoin (CBE)",
      "schema:inDefinedTermSet": {
        "@type": "schema:DefinedTermSet",
        "@id": "https://japan-cannabinoid-trends.routeflags.com/taxonomy/compound-cbe-scheme"
      },
      "schema:alternateName": [
        "カンナビエルソイン (CBE)",
        "CBE",
        "Cannabielsoin A"
      ],
      "schema:description": "`CBE` is the owner's search term for **cannabielsoin**. On 2026-11-11 the owner confirmed that CBE is intended to mean cannabielsoin. The chemical identity and identifiers were verified against the FDA GSRS approved record for CANNABIELSOIN (UNII 2ERD6V6652; CAS 52025-76-0; PubChem CID 162113; formula C21H30O3) and Wikidata Q22668702 (en label 'cannabielsoin', ja label 'カンナビエルソイン'). The owner's term and the query strings remain unchanged. Cannabielsoin is described as a non-enzymatic phytocannabinoid whose natural presence in Cannabis sativa has been established (Haghdoost et al. 2024, Biomedicines 12:1551, DOI 10.3390/biomedicines12071551); it is also an oxidation product and mammalian metabolite of cannabidiol (Yamamoto et al. 1988, PMID 3254981; 1989, PMID 2614640; 1991, PMID 1806944; Hartsel et al. 1983, DOI 10.1055/s-2007-969870). It is therefore placed in compound-minor, the category for phytocannabinoids other than the major pair. This is a route/origin placement, not a chemical-class claim: the chemical-class taxonomy has no CBE-type class, so no chemical-class link is asserted. The 2024 in-vitro CB1 study is cited only as pharmacological context, not as evidence about Japanese market presence. **Query limitation:** Google Trends and YouTube series for `CBE` / `CBE リキッド` measure occurrences of those literal strings. They do not verify that a result concerns cannabielsoin, and `CBE` is a short acronym that can match unrelated text. The existing samples are string-match observations, not proof of content relevance. Basis and source record: docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md (ISS-20261111-TAX-040 addendum).",
      "schema:termCode": "compound_cbe"
    },
    {
