      },
      "schema:description": "`Δ9-THCA-A` is the owner's search term, registered on 2026-11-11 (ISS-20261111-TAX-041) as one of three distinct search-topic concepts that replace the generic chemical interpretation of `THCA`; `compound_thca` remains the separate registration of the literal string `THCA`. The term is registered as a search string, not as a substance identification. The single-substance chemical identity remains UNCONFIRMED. The cited sources document the chemistry and nomenclature: Moreno-Sanz 2016 (DOI 10.1089/can.2016.0008) describes Δ9-tetrahydrocannabinolic acid A (2-carboxy-THC) as the acidic precursor of Δ9-THC, biosynthesized and accumulated in Cannabis glandular trichomes, and notes that the literature has used 'THCA' loosely for several acidic derivatives; Filer 2021 (DOI 10.1089/can.2021.0003, PMCID PMC8612408) treats THCA-A and THCA-B as distinct structural isomers. Those statements concern chemistry and nomenclature, not the content of any Google Trends or YouTube result and not any product sold under the term. This concept carries no skos:exactMatch and no CAS number, PubChem CID, UNII, ChEBI ID or Wikidata QID: this change did not verify or select a single external record for the registered string, and the owner's confirmation fixes the query topic, not result-level substance identity. It stays in compound_identity_unverified, an administrative registration-state category, so the placement asserts no route/origin, natural occurrence, chemical class, structure, synonym or regulatory status. No skos:related link to compound_thca, compound_thc or another new concept is asserted; the precursor relation to Δ9-THC is recorded in this note only. **Query limitation:** Google Trends and YouTube series for `Δ9-THCA-A` / `Δ9-THCA-A リキッド` measure occurrences of those literal strings; a matching result is not evidence of Δ9-THCA-A content. Evidence that would move the term out of this category: a verified external identifier for the named isomer (a documented chemical name with a verified identifier), after which the category that evidence supports can be assigned; the basis belongs in docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md.",
