      "schema:description": "`THCB-O-acetate` is the owner's search term, registered on 2026-11-11 (ISS-20261111-TAX-041) as one of three distinct search-topic concepts that replace the generic chemical interpretation of `THCB`; `compound_thcb` remains the separate registration of the literal string `THCB`. The term is registered as a search string, not as a substance identification. The single-substance chemical identity remains UNCONFIRMED. It is an isomer-unspecified acetate: it does not state Δ8 or Δ9, so it denotes no single substance and no single external record. The 2024 MHLW amendment (令和6年厚生労働省令第81号, promulgated 2024-05-01, in force 2024-05-11) defines generic structural groups: group 1 (6a,7,8,10a-tetrahydro, the Δ9 series) and group 2 (6a,7,10,10a-tetrahydro, the Δ8 series) each cover a C1 hydroxy or acetoxy group and a C3 straight-chain C3–C8 alkyl group with no other substituents; the attached list (別紙) explicitly lists Δ9-THCB-O-acetate (n=4) and Δ8-THCB-O-acetate (n=4) among the newly designated substances. That is a statutory structural definition covering both isomers; it is not a determination about any product, search result or the query string, and it is not a route, natural-occurrence, pharmacology or safety claim. The concept is not a synonym of THC-O-acetate (the C5 pentyl analogue, compound_thc_o) or any other ester, and no relation to compound_thcb, compound_thc_o or another new concept is asserted. This concept carries no skos:exactMatch and no CAS number, PubChem CID, UNII, ChEBI ID or Wikidata QID: no single external record denotes the family, and the per-isomer records belong in docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md, never on the concept. It stays in compound_identity_unverified, an administrative registration-state category, so the placement asserts no route/origin, natural occurrence, chemical class, pharmacology, safety or regulatory status. **Query limitation:** Google Trends and YouTube series for `THCB-O-acetate` / `THCB-O-acetate リキッド` measure occurrences of those literal strings; a matching result is not evidence of either acetate isomer. Evidence that would move the term out of this category: an owner-confirmed single isomer with a verified external identifier, or a recorded source naming a single substance and its route/origin; the basis belongs in docs/provenance/compound-taxonomy.md.",
